00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.700
- All right, oh, well, welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:09.810
My name's Tony Leiserowitz.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:10.740
I'm a faculty member

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.150
over at the Yale School of the Environment

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.200
where I direct the Yale Program

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.694
on Climate Change Communication.

00:00:14.694 --> 00:00:19.350
Here we're gonna be hearing
about this particular talk,

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.060
called Navigating the Future:

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.820
Harnessing Data-Driven
Insights on Climate Mobility

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.100
to Build a Common Agenda.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.480
Very pleased to welcome to Yale,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.820
two friends of mine who
I was just with last week

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.170
at Climate Week in New York,
doing a really big and exciting

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.650
and really important effort
to look at climate migration,

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.140
climate force displacement of peoples,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.770
and how do we begin to think
proactively about managing

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:47.640
what is likely to be many
millions of people being forced

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.040
to move either temporarily or permanently.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.040
So unfortunately the reality

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.840
of climate change is all too evident

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.610
over certainly this past summer

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.440
with all time record global temperatures.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.170
But this has been happening
now for many, many years

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.880
for those that have been paying attention

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.420
and it's only getting more intense.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:11.100
So this is an incredibly
time appropriate conversation

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.810
to be having and I'm just super excited

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.990
to welcome both of them
to Yale to help brief us

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.390
about some of the work they've been doing.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.610
Here they're gonna unpack the
efforts of the Global Center

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:24.360
for Climate Mobility,
which they have spearheaded

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.434
to further climate mobility
knowledge and solutions.

00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:30.690
They will underscore the
importance of data, modeling

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.430
and stakeholder engagement

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.920
to support proactive adaption strategies

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.130
for communities confronting
the climate crisis.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.510
And so they're really gonna be presenting

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.010
this larger approach

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:45.690
of the GCCM "Anticipate, Plan &amp; Transform"

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:47.490
and discuss the findings
and recommendations

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.470
of their Africa Climate
Mobility Initiative,

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.290
which is where they've started,
as well as ongoing work

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.620
to expand this in Latin
America and the Caribbean.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.780
So I'll let them describe
the project in more detail,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.120
but let me quickly introduce them.

