to how to take our data and our research

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:59.520
and bring it out into what's actionable

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:00.770
from a youth perspective.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:06.190
So looking at the
modeling and the research

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:09.333
and the consultations, what that leads to.

00:11:09.333 --> 00:11:11.040
Sarah was one of the lead authors,

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.690
so she's gonna talk more
about how this was done,

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:15.810
but it led to the African Shifts report

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:17.400
and the Agenda for Action.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:18.750
The report is online.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.820
There's a link there you can find after.

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:22.110
Sarah will also go through the details.

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:23.130
I'll not speak a lot about it,

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:26.160
but that is basically taking
all that information together

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.130
and putting out in a format
that hopefully is digestible

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:32.190
and not your usual UN report.

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:34.830
And also the Agenda for
Action that are the actions

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:37.593
that we're looking to
hopefully drive up until 2030.

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:41.760
And here for Africa I should
say that this is something

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:42.900
that's done for all the regions,

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.070
but this might look different

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:45.930
for the Greater Caribbean.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:48.030
For Africa this was the
agenda that was advanced

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:51.840
or put forward in this context,
is focused on our plan,

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:56.040
empower and transform and we
have eight different actions

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:57.480
that touches upon different parts

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:58.980
of what we think needs to be done

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:00.300
or not what we think needs to be done,

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:03.450
but people in this
consultations came forward with

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:06.840
as actionable things that we
need to see leading up to 2030

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.040
when it comes to climate
mobility in Africa.

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:12.870
Also want to mention this,

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:14.730
we tried to put all this information

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:16.380
on a digital platform as well.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:18.300
We not only is try, we actually did it

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:22.560
and it is a more digestible
way to communicate the stories

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.874
and storytelling is an important
part on how to address this

00:12:25.874 --> 00:12:28.200
and make this comprehensible,

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:31.050
'cause there is modeling
going up until 2050

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:33.840
but that's not always
easy to for to understand

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.460
what that means for people.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:38.820
So we have interviews, we have videos,

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.190
we have different
stories, we have the data.

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:42.330
The data is available there.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:44.010
You can go on and play with it yourself,

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:44.843
the different data sets

00:12:44.843 --> 00:12:48.090
and see how Africa will
be affected according

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:48.923
to the modeling.

00:12:48.923 --> 00:12:50.610
Everything is available
online and you can go

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:51.760
and check it out there.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.540
I wanna wrap up my part here
by talking a little bit about

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:59.460
how we bring this ecosystem together

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:01.470
and how we're trying to
advance the coalitions.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:03.360
So this is from COP last year where we had

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:06.210
the first climate mobility
pavilion trying to bring

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:09.030
the climate mobility
discussion into the COP space,

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:10.773
which has not been done before.

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.530
I don't wanna discredit,
there are certain actors

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:16.800
that of course have operated
in the climate conference space

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:18.840
but it's not been a dedicated pavilion.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:20.670
So trying to bring the ecosystem together,

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:23.088
we put this pavilion together
and had over 70 events

00:13:23.088 --> 00:13:26.130
over 10 days with everything
from heads of states

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:29.940
to communicate activists
participating in coming together.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:31.470
Also last week as Tony mentioned,

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:32.490
we had the chance to meet Tony

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:33.780
and Tony was one-on-one of the panels.

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.930
We had a climate mobility
summit on the margins

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:40.980
of the UN general assembly
bringing together same sort

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:43.680
of variation of stakeholders.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:45.450
You see Mayor Yvonne from Sierra Leon.

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:47.850
From Freetown, you see
the president of Guatemala

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:48.683
in the middle.

00:13:48.683 --> 00:13:52.920
You see the head of the
Association for Caribbean States,

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:54.930
Rodolfo Banque, standing
in the middle as well,

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:57.510
as well as various just UN entities

00:13:57.510 --> 00:13:59.400
and academic as well
from another institution.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:00.960
We don't have to talk
about which institution

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:03.090
but he's hiding up in the corner,

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:04.750
hopefully at Yale next time.

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:07.500
(Sarah chuckles)

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:11.850
- And with that we are
moving into the work

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:13.140
that we've done in Africa.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:17.610
Maybe just to dovetail
with the where David ended

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:20.340
and kind of, you know,
connect the dots a little bit.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:24.510
So this initiative has ambitions really

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:26.811
on three different levels.

00:14:26.811 --> 00:14:30.585
We started at the regional
level but we're working

00:14:30.585 --> 00:14:33.510
across different regions
to connect efforts

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:36.510
at the global level and at
the same time as I'll discuss

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:37.890
a little further going down

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:39.990
from the regional level
down to the national

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:40.920
and community level.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:43.380
So we are trying to really connect efforts

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:46.470
across these different
levels of governance

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:48.150
and as David already described,

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:52.050
also working across different
sectors of policymaking

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:54.570
as well as across different stakeholders.

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:57.780
So it's really kind of a
pretty intricate puzzle

00:14:57.780 --> 00:14:59.164
that we're trying to solve

00:14:59.164 --> 00:15:03.150
and it's I think on the one
hand driven by the urgency

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:05.850
of the climate crisis as Tony mentioned.

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:08.790
But I think it's also really
driven by our analysis

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:11.048
of the current geopolitical environment

00:15:11.048 --> 00:15:15.960
and the need to elevate the
voices of the global majority

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:18.300
in what is the climate conversation.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:23.300
So as David mentioned, we
have a discourse on climate

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:26.580
that's driven by mitigation
and decarbonization,

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:29.640
which is important but
for the majority of people

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.950
in the world that are most
affected by the climate crisis,

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:35.372
there's very little to
decarbonize in their lives.

00:15:35.372 --> 00:15:38.070
And so how do we elevate their concerns

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:39.900
in the global conversation?

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.753
That is very much what
drives kind of our work.

00:15:43.741 --> 00:15:46.920
For the Africa climate ability,

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.230
I actually don't have a
clicker so I'll have to ask you

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:52.320
to click forward, I'll go
a bit through our findings.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.030
David has explained the process.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:56.550
So we did modeling, we did field research,

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:59.730
we did consultations, we
put together a report.

00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:01.050
These are some of our findings

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:04.320
and I'll walk you walk you
through the substance now

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:05.630
and then we'll come back to more

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:07.473
of the process conversation.

00:16:08.901 --> 00:16:13.530
This is not our finding,
this is from the IPCC,

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:17.700
but I think it's fairly common knowledge.

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:19.380
We have this discrepancy

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:22.770
between Africa's greenhouse gas emissions,

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:23.820
what I just mentioned.

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:26.340
There are very few countries
that have, you know,

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:30.234
significant work to do on
decarbonization in Africa.

00:16:30.234 --> 00:16:32.940
But when we look at the climate risk,

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:35.610
it's very high for much of the continent.

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:38.550
So you have very little
contribution to the problem,

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:43.293
but very high exposure to and
vulnerability to the problem.

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.440
We also looked at, and this
is thanks to research done

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:52.320
by our colleague Dr. Nick
Simpson who was a co-author

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.936
of the report.

00:16:53.936 --> 00:16:56.640
We also looked at what is the level

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.875
of climate change literacy
across the continent.

00:16:59.875 --> 00:17:04.875
And this is based on Afrobarometer data

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:11.040
and they have looked at
the subnational level,

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:12.810
different districts what's the level

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:17.377
of climate change literacy and
found very high discrepancies

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:20.493
within countries and across countries.

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.420
And I think it dovetails
with some of the findings

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:26.790
that you presented Tony last week,

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:31.790
which is that you still
have quite low levels

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:35.643
of climate change knowledge in
many parts of the continent.

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:39.870
Generally the awareness is
higher in cities and urban areas.

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:42.270
It's lower in rural areas

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:45.993
and more remote kind of border regions.

00:17:48.261 --> 00:17:51.420
But that doesn't
necessarily always dovetail

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.180
with people's lived
experiences as we'll see

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:56.430
from our own field research.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:59.130
But basically if we are
just going with the kind of,

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:01.080
have people heard of climate change,

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:04.800
do they know what the impacts
might be on their livelihoods,

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:06.780
the rates are quite low,

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:09.213
especially in some of the
most vulnerable areas.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:16.260
So this is based on the both quantitative

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:17.790
and qualitative data collection.

00:18:17.790 --> 00:18:21.570
So we did surveys but also
interviews and this was done

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:24.003
by the mixed migration center for us.

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:28.980
In those seven locations,
David showed a map

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.200
with seven locations across the continent.

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.060
This is where this was done.

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:36.270
We had a mix of rural and urban areas,

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:39.390
coastal, different types
of climate, impacts

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:42.270
that were already occurring,
some areas affected by drought,

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:46.170
others experiencing
cyclones, sea level rise,

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:48.093
different types of climate impacts.

00:18:49.155 --> 00:18:54.155
And what we found across
those seven locations

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:56.760
was that overall the majority

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:00.060
of people very much
notice climate disruptions

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:00.893
in their lives.

00:19:00.893 --> 00:19:03.049
Like they're aware that this is happening

00:19:03.049 --> 00:19:07.890
but it's not something that they consider

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:10.410
or most of them do not consider to move

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.690
because of those disruptions.

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:17.610
So for a majority more than
50% they say, "Yeah, you know,

00:19:17.610 --> 00:19:19.290
we see stuff is happening,

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:21.967
we take certain measures to try to adapt,

00:19:21.967 --> 00:19:25.440
but moving is not necessarily
one that we would wanna take

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:27.360
or are considering taking."

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:31.470
You have almost a quarter
though that say, "Yeah,

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:34.920
we would maybe move if we
had the resources to do so."

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:38.610
So the constraint on
moving has often has to do

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:40.920
with the resources that
people have available

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:43.683
or their own perception
of those resources.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:52.652
If we look at reasons
for moving, you can see,

00:19:52.652 --> 00:19:55.320
and this is not surprising
I think from the literature,

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:59.134
but it confirms a lot of existing research

00:19:59.134 --> 00:20:03.120
that very much climate change
interacts with other factors

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:07.530
that make people want to
move, consider moving.

00:20:07.530 --> 00:20:10.470
And here we looked at economic impacts.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:12.752
So livelihood factors most of the time,

00:20:12.752 --> 00:20:17.752
and you see that in most of
the cases it's more livelihood

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:19.560
or income related reasons

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:23.970
that would make people consider
moving or want to move.

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:26.190
The outliers are really in areas

00:20:26.190 --> 00:20:30.180
where you had fairly recent
extreme events like Beira,

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:32.707
for example, which was hit by cyclone

00:20:32.707 --> 00:20:33.540
and I think it's covered here,

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:37.147
but had a very fairly high
percentage of people saying,

00:20:37.147 --> 00:20:39.060
"Yes, we wanna move."

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:41.100
Similarly, Chihuahua in Malawi,

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:45.150
which was like hit by
different impacts alternating

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:46.500
between flooding and drought.

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:49.260
And so where people
were kind of at a point

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:50.430
where they were like, "Okay,

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:52.380
we are ready to move because of this

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:55.830
because it's just been too much whiplash."

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:57.150
But generally, for example,

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:02.150
in a Lagos or Alexandria where
people had some perception

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:05.040
of climate impacts,

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:07.710
it was mostly about
like living conditions,

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:10.170
how can we move to a better neighborhood?

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:13.413
But it was less driven
by the climate factor.

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:21.660
This is the gender breakdown.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:25.743
And what we found across locations,

00:21:27.891 --> 00:21:28.724
it's not always true,

00:21:28.724 --> 00:21:32.340
but mostly women were more
reluctant to move than men.

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:34.713
So they had less intentions to move.

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:41.810
And if you go to the next
slide, this is by age group,

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:47.700
we found that the young
people were those most prone

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:48.840
to wanting to move.

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:51.127
So the majority of
young people still said,

00:21:51.127 --> 00:21:53.689
"I'm not considering moving,"

00:21:53.689 --> 00:21:58.689
but they were the largest age
group that had actually plans

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:04.760
to move were more actively
engaged in making those plans.

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:10.133
Next slide.

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:15.360
So then we go to the quantitative work,

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:17.760
and this is based on the modeling

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:21.150
that David briefly presented.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:23.130
We did two types of modeling.

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:25.840
We looked at cross-border migration

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:28.140
and we looked at internal movements

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:31.680
and we did not manage to do
both within the same model.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:33.393
So we had two separate models.

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:38.580
This is the findings for
the cross-border movement.

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:40.650
And this was a pilot I would say.

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:43.150
So the methodology was quite new

00:22:44.468 --> 00:22:49.468
and the results are I
think fairly significant.

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:55.200
But also, you know, in the context

00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:57.120
of the overall population development

00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:59.190
in Africa, not overwhelming.

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:04.190
So you have a fairly small percentage

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:08.430
of overall projected
movement being related

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:09.480
to climate change, right?

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:14.480
Like this is the total projection
of cross border migrants

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:17.475
that could be moving by 2050.

00:23:17.475 --> 00:23:21.600
And then within that the
climate related movement

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:23.500
is a fairly small percentage

00:23:24.791 --> 00:23:29.791
and, sorry, this is the
more optimistic scenario

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:34.770
in terms of climate impacts

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:38.280
and this is the more pessimistic scenario,

00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:40.477
so you'll also see that actually

00:23:40.477 --> 00:23:45.477
the impact of climate change
have a more reducing impact

00:23:46.110 --> 00:23:47.520
on cross border mobility,

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:50.760
which makes sense again if we
look at the need for people

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:52.533
to have resources to move.

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:55.713
Next slide.

00:23:57.360 --> 00:23:59.850
Then we have internal movements,

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:01.890
so people moving within countries

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:06.890
and that is a more significant
amount of people projected

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:12.740
to be moving anywhere
between 46 to 113 million.

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:19.490
So if we stay within the
scenarios, the most likely outcome

00:24:22.260 --> 00:24:25.740
under a kind of high emissions future

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:28.470
is around 88 million people,

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:32.250
4% of the African population
moving within their countries.

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:34.846
But it could go up to 113.

00:24:34.846 --> 00:24:39.660
A more optimistic scenario
when it comes to development

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:41.850
in climate emissions sees

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:45.093
a lower range of internal mobility.

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:51.900
And this is the hotspots analysis.

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:56.760
So one thing is to try to
put numbers on the movement.

00:24:56.760 --> 00:25:00.630
I think the more important maybe potential

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:03.120
of this analysis lies with understanding

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:05.083
the geographic distribution

00:25:05.083 --> 00:25:09.660
of why are those movements
likely to happen?

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:12.330
So what this map shows are areas

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:15.420
that are likely to be origins

00:25:15.420 --> 00:25:19.608
or destinations respectively of movements.

00:25:19.608 --> 00:25:22.680
And you see that they're
quite concentrated

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:27.680
in the Sahel belt on the coasts

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:31.020
and then also on some
of the border regions

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:35.520
like Lake Victoria, for example,
you have the border region

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:38.913
between Ethiopia, Sudan, Eritrea.

00:25:42.095 --> 00:25:46.680
So this is what we'll be
using going forward working

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:50.640
with governments at the
more national, sub-regional

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:53.523
and local level is hotspots analysis.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:59.430
So this is the next step after we've done

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:01.350
the continental level analysis

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:04.180
and had kind of have presented our results

00:26:04.180 --> 00:26:05.687
and Agenda for Action,

00:26:05.687 --> 00:26:10.522
we are now working with
individual countries,

00:26:10.522 --> 00:26:13.457
sub-regional organizations and cities

00:26:13.457 --> 00:26:18.457
to take these hotspots
analysis down to the country,

00:26:18.780 --> 00:26:23.780
local sub-regional level to
help them start making sense

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:29.660
of prospective population
shifts within their territory

00:26:30.447 --> 00:26:35.447
and how our analysis
cross references with data

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:39.360
that they may be having at
the local and national level.

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:41.880
So we're trying to integrate
different sources of data

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:43.710
and knowledge at the national level,

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:48.710
at the local level with a
view to supporting planning,

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:54.933
policy development at
that level of intervention

00:26:54.933 --> 00:26:59.460
and eventually drive towards
projects and programming

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:01.503
in those hotspots areas.

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:08.583
So the first country where
we're doing this is Uganda.

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:13.260
This is the steps of the
process and it mirrors very much

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:14.820
the kind of process we've gone through

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:15.870
at the regional level.

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:19.560
So we start with this
analysis of different data

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:23.550
and research, bringing
together our modeling data

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:26.793
with other sources of
data and information.

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:30.930
We go through a phase of consultations now

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:35.460
within the country and
then want to develop

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:36.750
what we call a blueprint.

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:37.583
This could, I think,

00:27:37.583 --> 00:27:40.380
take different forms depending
on the country context,

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:44.730
but the ultimate goal is to
influence both adaptation

00:27:44.730 --> 00:27:46.740
and development planning that's happening

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:48.060
at the country level

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:52.020
with this information about
like where are the areas,

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:54.682
the geographies, but
also the groups of people

00:27:54.682 --> 00:27:57.030
and the sectors of your economy

00:27:57.030 --> 00:27:59.280
that you should be
particularly concerned about

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:01.443
when it comes to people moving.

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:07.830
This is from the climate mobility profile

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:10.950
we have pulled together for Uganda,

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:13.770
starting with climate information,

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:16.473
what are the trends in the country?

00:28:17.425 --> 00:28:22.425
Next slide, what are the hazards

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:25.200
and how are people affected by them?

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:29.043
So for Uganda you see
different types of hazards,

00:28:30.985 --> 00:28:32.490
you don't see this here,

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:35.760
but they're affecting
different parts of the country.

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:36.593
Next slide.

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:38.580
Then again we are looking

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:41.880
at the climate literacy rates based

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:46.563
on the data we used also for
the continental level analysis,

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:50.850
so understanding different
levels of climate literacy

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:55.850
across the country and then
we're drawing on our own data

00:28:57.930 --> 00:29:01.260
to look at what's the
situation for Uganda?

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:06.120
So how will cross border
flows for Uganda look like

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:09.660
over the next 30 years?

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:11.073
Here you can see.

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:15.013
But much more movement projected

00:29:15.013 --> 00:29:18.963
to go out of the country
than coming into the country.

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:22.293
Next slide.

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:27.090
Internal projections.

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:29.430
This is what we saw

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:31.140
for the continental level.

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:32.550
Here in Uganda,

00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:37.550
the high point is 5.1
million potential internally,

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:40.563
displaced people or people moving.

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:46.923
And this is the hotspots map for Uganda.

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:55.470
One is the kind of most likely
where different scenarios

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:58.560
of the modeling have the
highest level of agreement.

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:00.303
This is how you get to this map.

00:30:02.713 --> 00:30:07.080
And this map is the actual
numbers of people projected

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:10.290
to move from one part of the country

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:12.153
to another part of the country.

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:17.070
And those pixels are roughly four

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:19.193
to five square kilometers.

00:30:19.193 --> 00:30:21.213
So that's the resolution.

00:30:24.690 --> 00:30:27.150
Then we look at what are existing factors

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:30.180
of vulnerability such as
the climate literacy rates,

00:30:30.180 --> 00:30:34.114
but also where are, for
example, existing refugee camps

00:30:34.114 --> 00:30:36.783
or refugee populations?

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:40.480
So looking at them as a group of concern

00:30:41.610 --> 00:30:42.900
but also looking at things

00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:46.577
like poverty levels and marginalization.

00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:50.763
Next slide.

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:56.720
And this is our analysis by cities.

00:30:57.360 --> 00:31:01.698
So we have different ways
of looking at the data.

00:31:01.698 --> 00:31:02.531
For the continental level,

00:31:02.531 --> 00:31:06.300
we did a geographic regions analysis,

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:09.600
we looked at urban areas,
we looked at rural areas,

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:11.070
coastal areas separately.

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:14.130
And we're doing the same
at the country level.

00:31:14.130 --> 00:31:18.210
So this is the city level analysis,

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:21.450
which are the cities that will be growing

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:24.000
because there's overall urbanization

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:25.950
and population growth going on,

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:30.950
but where climate mobility
could be leading to a dent

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:31.920
in that growth.

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:34.800
So basically you have
cities that are growing

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:36.570
but because of climate impacts,

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:41.010
there are some people forecast
predicted to leave that city,

00:31:41.010 --> 00:31:44.040
which kind of suggests
that overall conditions

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:46.740
in the city could become more
hazardous also for people

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:48.213
who are not leaving.

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:53.700
And then you have cities
that are projected

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:56.760
to grow due to climate mobility.

00:31:56.760 --> 00:32:01.760
So where climate mobility

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:04.200
is actually contributing meaningfully

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:06.480
to the growth of the city.

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:10.113
And you see kind of like this
flip side here in Uganda.

00:32:13.650 --> 00:32:14.483
Next slide.

00:32:15.630 --> 00:32:19.233
So what are we doing
next with this analysis?

00:32:20.578 --> 00:32:23.940
We're planning to have consultations,

00:32:23.940 --> 00:32:26.220
engagement with
stakeholders across Uganda.

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:29.130
So going to different parts of the country

00:32:29.130 --> 00:32:32.010
to share our initial findings,

00:32:32.010 --> 00:32:34.720
but to understand what
are the local realities

00:32:36.007 --> 00:32:40.230
and how does this inter
intersect with what's going on?

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:42.810
How people are perceiving climate impacts

00:32:42.810 --> 00:32:46.350
and the movement of
people, the implications

00:32:46.350 --> 00:32:48.520
that already resolved at the moment

00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:55.160
with a view to co-developing
recommendations for action,

00:32:55.479 --> 00:32:58.500
looking at what could
be pilot interventions

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:01.920
in areas that are particularly impacted,

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:06.510
and then also seeing at
how do we get to a level

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:11.510
of granularity that could
actually support decision making

00:33:11.670 --> 00:33:16.670
like if you wanted to decide
to put water infrastructure

00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:20.220
in a certain place or
to build more housing

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:23.310
in a certain place, the modeling

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:27.728
that we have is not sufficiently detailed

00:33:27.728 --> 00:33:31.530
and you see it has these
large uncertainty intervals.

00:33:31.530 --> 00:33:33.960
So, you know, we wouldn't be like, "Yeah,

00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:35.640
you should really put your housing here

00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:38.340
because our model says you know this."

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:39.750
So the idea is also like

00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:43.620
how do we start based on
more locally collected data

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:46.080
to build decision support tools,

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:47.820
which would be more agent-based models?

00:33:47.820 --> 00:33:49.650
So like actually understanding

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:51.540
what are the local dynamics

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:54.460
and how do we develop support tools

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:58.950
that can actually inform these kinds

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:02.099
of infrastructure investment decisions,

00:34:02.099 --> 00:34:07.099
policy making at a more
local and granular level?

00:34:11.700 --> 00:34:12.533
Yep.

00:34:15.660 --> 00:34:17.610
I know we kind of like
take a step back up.

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:19.560
So in Africa we've kind of gone

00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:23.729
from the continental down
to the country and local.

00:34:23.729 --> 00:34:26.100
In the Greater Caribbean,

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:29.770
we are starting where we
started in Africa two years ago

00:34:32.430 --> 00:34:36.150
with an effort at kind
of mobilizing interest

00:34:36.150 --> 00:34:40.770
and political buy-in for the initiative,

00:34:40.770 --> 00:34:43.293
which was officially launched last year.

00:34:44.190 --> 00:34:48.330
So we've been holding a number of events,

00:34:48.330 --> 00:34:51.210
just socializing this
idea and this initiative

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:53.133
with governments in the region.

00:34:55.200 --> 00:34:57.900
We have started modeling and we're using

00:34:57.900 --> 00:34:59.730
a different approach this time.

00:34:59.730 --> 00:35:04.230
So the model that we
use for Africa is called

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:05.310
a gravity model.

00:35:05.310 --> 00:35:07.440
It's based mainly focused

00:35:07.440 --> 00:35:10.816
on kind of geographic assumptions about

00:35:10.816 --> 00:35:13.940
how people are distributed in space

00:35:13.940 --> 00:35:18.450
and then when climate impacts
kind of are layered in,

00:35:18.450 --> 00:35:22.260
how does that affect the attractiveness

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:25.560
of places vis-a-vis each other?

00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:28.440
This is an econometric model
we are using this time.

00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:29.730
So it looks much more

00:35:29.730 --> 00:35:33.240
at how climate impacts will
affect different sectors

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:36.090
of the economy and labor
productivity, and different sectors

00:35:36.090 --> 00:35:40.770
of the economy, and how that
will affect people's decisions

00:35:40.770 --> 00:35:45.470
to go to certain areas or
move out of other areas.

00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:50.470
So we're expecting to have quite
different types of results.

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:54.660
It will still give us an idea of again,

00:35:54.660 --> 00:35:57.030
like what are hotspots areas,

00:35:57.030 --> 00:36:00.780
but it will be much more tied
to what is the economic impact

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:01.650
of climate change

00:36:01.650 --> 00:36:04.983
and how is this affecting
people's decisions to move?

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:08.760
And this is our roadmap.

00:36:08.760 --> 00:36:11.697
We're working towards COP 29,

00:36:11.697 --> 00:36:14.010
so that means end of 2024

00:36:14.010 --> 00:36:17.490
to basically arrive at what David laid out

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.880
the process of first doing the research,

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:23.880
then doing the consultations,
having a report,

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:26.877
having stories, bringing it all to COP

00:36:26.877 --> 00:36:30.390
and then again like in
that region as well,

00:36:30.390 --> 00:36:33.000
starting to work with
individual champion countries

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:34.800
to look at what does this mean for you?

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:37.313
How do we bring this
down to the local level

00:36:37.313 --> 00:36:41.230
while at the same time
continuing to build that momentum

00:36:42.456 --> 00:36:44.883
in global policy forum.

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:48.600
So that was a lot. (chuckles)

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:49.773
Now the floor is yours.

00:36:51.414 --> 00:36:52.826
- Great.

00:36:52.826 --> 00:36:55.326
(crowd claps)

00:36:58.200 --> 00:37:02.373
Questions, comments
also, (chuckles) funny.

00:37:04.740 --> 00:37:05.573
- [Crowd Member] Thank you so much

00:37:05.573 --> 00:37:06.720
for this great presentation.

00:37:06.720 --> 00:37:08.490
I just have a quick question
going back to your point

00:37:08.490 --> 00:37:11.130
on using different models
for different regions,

00:37:11.130 --> 00:37:12.750
would you mind explaining
a little bit more

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:15.660
of why you decide your team decided to use

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:17.250
the gravity based model for Africa

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:22.250
and then the more sectorial
economic-based approach

00:37:22.470 --> 00:37:25.470
for campaigns and maybe maybe
different kinds of models

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:27.870
you use for other regions
do your project cover?

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:32.343
- Yeah, should I go ahead?

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:38.550
The main reason was that for
the gravity model to work well,

00:37:38.550 --> 00:37:43.364
you basically need some territory
to move people around on

00:37:43.364 --> 00:37:45.950
(chuckles) and with a
lot of the small islands

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:48.060
in the Caribbean, it's very difficult.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:53.060
Like the model is struggling
to basically project movements

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:55.563
if the territory is very small.

00:37:57.000 --> 00:37:59.400
And so I think the search

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:01.560
for a different alternative model led us

00:38:01.560 --> 00:38:03.690
to this econometric model.

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:05.700
We had a workshop with all kinds

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:08.640
of different modelers
presenting different approaches

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:10.710
that they're currently pursuing.

00:38:10.710 --> 00:38:13.440
What we are looking for I think is always

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:16.470
an approach that is globally applicable,

00:38:16.470 --> 00:38:18.030
which limits options.

00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:21.450
So we are trying to find
models that can work

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:23.760
across different regions ultimately.

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:28.760
So we I think are looking, yeah,

00:38:28.950 --> 00:38:32.400
to as much as possible get
inputs in terms of the data

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:33.690
that are region specific,

00:38:33.690 --> 00:38:36.060
but have a model that can be applied

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:37.773
across different contexts.

00:38:39.600 --> 00:38:40.800
Yeah, but the main reason

00:38:40.800 --> 00:38:44.220
was that we were trying to look
at these small island states

00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:47.580
and the gravity model at
according to our colleagues

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:51.030
who are doing the modeling is not so good

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:54.900
at looking at population redistribution

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:56.793
in these very small territories.

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:03.541
- Could you speak

00:39:03.541 --> 00:39:05.790
a little bit more about
the decision support tools

00:39:05.790 --> 00:39:09.150
that sort of are a need
now and how they interplay

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:10.986
with the current models that you have

00:39:10.986 --> 00:39:13.830
and like what you would
hope that they would answer?

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:16.830
- Yeah, so I think again,

00:39:16.830 --> 00:39:20.100
like our model gives kind
of an indication of, okay,

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:22.710
these are areas that you
should be concerned about

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:26.370
or look at for action. (chuckles)

00:39:26.370 --> 00:39:29.820
But then when it comes to
actually guiding decisions

00:39:29.820 --> 00:39:34.820
on, you know, again putting in
place specific infrastructure

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:39.930
or maybe expanding social
protection programs

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:40.763
or what have you,

00:39:40.763 --> 00:39:45.210
like actually kind of a
better understanding of, well,

00:39:45.210 --> 00:39:48.090
how many people when are
they going to move based

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:51.383
on which triggers or thresholds?

00:39:51.383 --> 00:39:54.240
You need something that is more informed

00:39:54.240 --> 00:39:56.610
by what's actually really
going on in the country.

00:39:56.610 --> 00:39:58.500
Because what we know is that the model

00:39:58.500 --> 00:40:02.040
that we have is informed
by global climate models

00:40:02.040 --> 00:40:05.970
that often struggle to represent
what's happening in Africa

00:40:05.970 --> 00:40:07.980
because there's not so
much data collection

00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:09.828
and data available.

00:40:09.828 --> 00:40:12.930
So you have often
assumptions being made also

00:40:12.930 --> 00:40:15.630
on the migration side about
like, well, let's, you know,

00:40:15.630 --> 00:40:19.230
use Ghana and expand the data from Ghana

00:40:19.230 --> 00:40:20.430
across these other five countries

00:40:20.430 --> 00:40:22.880
for which we don't have
data, that kind of thing.

00:40:23.792 --> 00:40:25.800
Which then if you are actually

00:40:25.800 --> 00:40:28.380
in one of those other five
countries is not so helpful,

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:31.860
right, your model is
not informed by the data

00:40:31.860 --> 00:40:34.860
that actually is reflecting
the local reality.

00:40:34.860 --> 00:40:36.660
And so if you want something

00:40:36.660 --> 00:40:40.200
that can actually support
decisions on like should we build

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:43.140
a sea wall here, or put water pipes there,

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:46.770
or build housing here, you need something

00:40:46.770 --> 00:40:50.144
that reflects more like
actual dynamics in the country

00:40:50.144 --> 00:40:53.700
and you have companies and consultancies

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:55.113
that do that kind of work.

00:40:56.250 --> 00:40:57.780
So we are trying to see, you know,

00:40:57.780 --> 00:41:01.890
once we have our more broader
analysis of like, okay,

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:02.723
you know, we know

00:41:02.723 --> 00:41:06.180
that these coastal areas you
should be concerned about,

00:41:06.180 --> 00:41:09.990
how do we bring that expertise
to our government partners

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:13.140
to then say, okay, let's do
the more granular analysis

00:41:13.140 --> 00:41:14.250
of, you know,

00:41:14.250 --> 00:41:17.670
you might wanna put this piece
of infrastructure in this way

00:41:17.670 --> 00:41:21.780
or, yeah, so to actually
kind of like get started

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:23.433
on specific projects.

00:41:27.180 --> 00:41:28.050
Yep.

00:41:28.050 --> 00:41:30.480
- Yeah, so thank you both so much.

00:41:30.480 --> 00:41:34.260
Aside from the suggestions or the action

00:41:34.260 --> 00:41:37.560
that the results of these models
might, you know, point out

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:41.340
for the countries that
will experience, you know,

00:41:41.340 --> 00:41:44.460
the harshest effects
of the climate crisis,

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:48.040
I guess what have you found
in terms of showing this data

00:41:48.911 --> 00:41:51.382
to the countries that
are the biggest causes

00:41:51.382 --> 00:41:54.810
and how do you feel as
if this kind of a finding

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:59.220
or these kinds of reports are
moving the needle politically

00:41:59.220 --> 00:42:01.743
or is it just another drop in the ocean?

00:42:03.819 --> 00:42:04.652
- That's a good question.

00:42:04.652 --> 00:42:08.420
I mean, I think the main
concern usually for countries

00:42:08.420 --> 00:42:10.350
in the global north,

00:42:10.350 --> 00:42:13.500
if you will is is more around
like who's coming to us,

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:16.740
right, like do you have
figures for Africans moving

00:42:16.740 --> 00:42:21.093
to Europe or Latin
Americans moving to the US?

00:42:22.073 --> 00:42:26.280
I think what we are trying
to convey is the message

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:29.940
that, you know, most people
are moving close to home,

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:31.080
which is already true,

00:42:31.080 --> 00:42:33.900
but will most likely be
true in the future too

00:42:33.900 --> 00:42:36.120
from what we know and what we observe.

00:42:36.120 --> 00:42:39.240
So, yes, there will be
also south north movements,

00:42:39.240 --> 00:42:43.560
but the majority of movement
is happening close to home

00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:47.733
and people are not all, you
know, clamoring to leave.

00:42:48.930 --> 00:42:53.930
And so really what we're trying
to, I think pinpoint is A

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:57.540
the need for scaling up
adaptation investment,

00:42:57.540 --> 00:43:01.290
which is not a new message,
but to reinforce that point

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:05.460
but then also to guide
that investment, right?

00:43:05.460 --> 00:43:08.490
Like to say if you are trying to, again,

00:43:08.490 --> 00:43:10.544
in a more anticipatory fashion,

00:43:10.544 --> 00:43:14.580
not just kind of try to
get on top of disasters

00:43:14.580 --> 00:43:19.580
and do recovery on a perpetual basis,

00:43:20.220 --> 00:43:21.960
but to try to look ahead

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:25.200
and see how can we get ahead of the curve

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:28.440
in certain instances and say, "Okay,

00:43:28.440 --> 00:43:30.660
this is where people are likely to move

00:43:30.660 --> 00:43:33.690
if we can put investment
here now, you know,

00:43:33.690 --> 00:43:36.540
then the city does not
have to become overwhelmed

00:43:36.540 --> 00:43:37.770
with informal settlements

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:39.540
where living conditions are terrible.

00:43:39.540 --> 00:43:42.597
You can start putting
infrastructure in place now,"

00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:47.850
you can start, you know,
thinking about economic sectors

00:43:47.850 --> 00:43:50.433
where people could find jobs now.

00:43:51.390 --> 00:43:53.190
So that is a little bit the logic

00:43:53.190 --> 00:43:57.000
on what we're hoping to
achieve is basically,

00:43:57.000 --> 00:44:01.496
A, get to a clear message
around like, you know,

00:44:01.496 --> 00:44:04.950
get out of your navel gazing
(chuckles) if you will.

00:44:04.950 --> 00:44:06.960
You know, the problems are acute

00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:11.037
and they're for people already real now

00:44:11.037 --> 00:44:14.340
and then, you know, start planning ahead

00:44:14.340 --> 00:44:16.860
and supporting countries

00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:20.253
before the situation becomes a crisis.

00:44:25.110 --> 00:44:26.460
- Could you say a bit more,

00:44:26.460 --> 00:44:29.040
I'm just thinking back in
the Africa model and the maps

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:30.270
that you had of the places

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:32.190
where people would be be fleeing from

00:44:32.190 --> 00:44:34.030
and where they would be going to,

00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:35.970
so the red versus the green locations,

00:44:35.970 --> 00:44:38.700
what variables are associated
or seem to be predictive

00:44:38.700 --> 00:44:40.710
of either of those two locations

00:44:40.710 --> 00:44:43.413
where people would be
moving from or moving to?

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:48.450
- Yeah, so the model generally assumes

00:44:48.450 --> 00:44:53.450
that people will be drawn to
more densely populated areas.

00:44:54.330 --> 00:44:57.390
So it has kind of like a
bias towards urbanization,

00:44:57.390 --> 00:44:59.490
assuming that people will wanna be

00:44:59.490 --> 00:45:00.840
where there's more amenities

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:04.336
and that those places are more attractive.

00:45:04.336 --> 00:45:08.790
But then basically what it does
is like it looks at a future

00:45:08.790 --> 00:45:11.820
where you have just development happening

00:45:11.820 --> 00:45:13.140
and where would people be

00:45:13.140 --> 00:45:14.880
in terms of population distribution.

00:45:14.880 --> 00:45:17.850
And then it compares that to a scenario

00:45:17.850 --> 00:45:20.790
where you have climate impacts happening

00:45:20.790 --> 00:45:25.230
and the climate impacts
are on water availability,

00:45:25.230 --> 00:45:29.130
on crop productivity, and are
on rangeland productivity.

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:34.130
So to cover kind of grazing
livestock-based livelihoods.

00:45:35.850 --> 00:45:39.753
And those are the main kind
of factors that influence

00:45:39.753 --> 00:45:42.780
the attractiveness of
places in the future.

00:45:42.780 --> 00:45:47.280
So you'll see basically the
model moving people out of areas

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:50.460
that where water availability
goes down and into areas

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:52.263
where water availability goes up.

00:45:53.490 --> 00:45:58.490
We've tried to capture things
like conflict in the model,

00:45:58.770 --> 00:46:01.890
but it's very imperfect
at this stage in the sense

00:46:01.890 --> 00:46:05.370
that you cannot predict
conflict into the future.

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:07.800
So you kind of have to
assume like certain areas

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:11.010
that had conflict in the
past will be less attractive

00:46:11.010 --> 00:46:12.480
because of conflict in the future,

00:46:12.480 --> 00:46:14.970
but we know that that's
not really how it works,

00:46:14.970 --> 00:46:17.527
where like the conflict may
be moving somewhere else.

00:46:17.527 --> 00:46:19.860
So that's a limitation.

00:46:19.860 --> 00:46:23.867
And I think it does
account to a certain extent

00:46:28.590 --> 00:46:32.625
for existing mobility patterns,

00:46:32.625 --> 00:46:36.390
but it obviously it cannot
capture all the intricacies

00:46:36.390 --> 00:46:39.120
of like family ties and ethnic ties

00:46:39.120 --> 00:46:41.880
and, you know, like that drive
a lot of people's decisions

00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:43.233
for where they're gonna go.

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:46.440
- [Crowd Member] So
there's actually current,

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:49.260
contemporary data on
how people already move

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:52.064
within some of these
locations that you can draw on

00:46:52.064 --> 00:46:54.540
as an input into the model.

00:46:54.540 --> 00:46:57.510
- Well, the problem is that
I think you could do that

00:46:57.510 --> 00:47:01.140
for these more smaller
scale agent based models.

00:47:01.140 --> 00:47:03.630
So if you wanted to actually
really say like, okay,

00:47:03.630 --> 00:47:06.270
we're in Accra, Ghana
and we wanna understand

00:47:06.270 --> 00:47:08.400
how might people be moving into Accra?

00:47:08.400 --> 00:47:10.830
I think you could start
like really looking at

00:47:10.830 --> 00:47:12.690
what are the existing migration patterns

00:47:12.690 --> 00:47:14.760
and how do we build a model based on that.

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:17.370
But for these kind of like
models that are trying to do this

00:47:17.370 --> 00:47:22.370
at scale, I mean, you don't
have that data at scale, right?

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:26.520
So that's the incompatibility if you will,

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:29.340
like that you need data that is available

00:47:29.340 --> 00:47:31.380
across different locations

00:47:31.380 --> 00:47:35.730
to have these kind of global models

00:47:35.730 --> 00:47:39.360
or whole of continent models.

00:47:39.360 --> 00:47:40.670
- But as also I think it's important

00:47:40.670 --> 00:47:42.900
to have the different
stages of the research

00:47:42.900 --> 00:47:44.070
as well with the modeling.

00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:46.500
That takes it down to the
other parts as well with both

00:47:48.094 --> 00:47:49.770
the qualitative part and the consultation,

00:47:49.770 --> 00:47:51.900
but also then, yeah, why it's important.

00:47:51.900 --> 00:47:54.240
you might say then like,
"You've done the work,

00:47:54.240 --> 00:47:56.460
why you go to the national
level, local level?"

00:47:56.460 --> 00:47:58.800
Of course that sounds like,
but it is really important

00:47:58.800 --> 00:48:00.772
to understand the real drivers of this,

00:48:00.772 --> 00:48:02.400
to talk to those people.

00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:04.500
I think that that's the
experience as well to really get

00:48:04.500 --> 00:48:07.860
a sense of, yeah, a
decision to move is not easy

00:48:07.860 --> 00:48:11.190
and it's usually not as
you cannot summarize it

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:14.700
as is one point driving it,
it's usually more complex

00:48:14.700 --> 00:48:16.953
and few points going into it.

00:48:22.170 --> 00:48:23.877
- [Crowd Member] So thank
you for this presentation.

00:48:23.877 --> 00:48:28.877
And I have also like a question
related to the assumptions

00:48:29.160 --> 00:48:30.783
that you have.

00:48:31.770 --> 00:48:35.340
So every model, the
result of a model depends

00:48:35.340 --> 00:48:36.990
on the assumption that you're taking.

00:48:36.990 --> 00:48:39.388
So if you can tell us
a little bit more about

00:48:39.388 --> 00:48:42.750
how you are gathering that information

00:48:42.750 --> 00:48:45.910
and what challenges you
have encountered as well

00:48:48.000 --> 00:48:50.070
in order to building that

00:48:50.070 --> 00:48:52.980
and how to calibrate
basically also with things

00:48:52.980 --> 00:48:56.130
that you see in the future.

00:48:56.130 --> 00:48:58.020
And the other question that I have

00:48:58.020 --> 00:49:02.340
is that, so you have these
ranges of probabilities

00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:06.930
of the forecast that the results

00:49:06.930 --> 00:49:10.140
that you can see, are you
building with that as well,

00:49:10.140 --> 00:49:12.210
like different scenarios depending

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:15.600
on how extreme you can
see or certain parameters

00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:19.552
that go after some
level as well, you know,

00:49:19.552 --> 00:49:24.016
to have the know the
recommendation or the work

00:49:24.016 --> 00:49:29.016
that you are gonna be working
with the different government?

00:49:29.127 --> 00:49:30.450
- I'm not sure I understood

00:49:30.450 --> 00:49:32.280
the second part of your question.

00:49:32.280 --> 00:49:37.280
On the first part, so we
didn't build the model.

00:49:37.440 --> 00:49:41.070
We work with colleagues
who are on the one hand

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:44.155
at City University of New
York and Columbia University

00:49:44.155 --> 00:49:47.190
and they built this model originally

00:49:47.190 --> 00:49:51.390
for the World Bank's Groundswell reports

00:49:51.390 --> 00:49:53.640
and then upgraded it for our purposes.

00:49:53.640 --> 00:49:55.200
So we built in,

00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:58.918
they used different population
data for this model.

00:49:58.918 --> 00:50:03.210
They used more layers
of climate information.

00:50:03.210 --> 00:50:06.330
So they put in a flood mask for example.

00:50:06.330 --> 00:50:08.483
They added this conflict mask.

00:50:08.483 --> 00:50:11.610
So there were more factors layered in

00:50:11.610 --> 00:50:14.266
than that was the case
for the Groundswell model.

00:50:14.266 --> 00:50:16.950
And the calibration as far as I know,

00:50:16.950 --> 00:50:19.800
was done basically picking again,

00:50:19.800 --> 00:50:22.080
countries for which data
is available and looking at

00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:25.680
in the past how have
certain things like drought

00:50:25.680 --> 00:50:28.020
or flooding affected migration

00:50:28.020 --> 00:50:30.663
and use that to kind
of predict the future.

00:50:31.906 --> 00:50:34.505
So, yeah, there's lots of kind of things

00:50:34.505 --> 00:50:39.505
where, you know, you say
okay, again, you know,

00:50:40.619 --> 00:50:41.970
what we find for Cameroon and Ghana

00:50:41.970 --> 00:50:44.862
and five other countries
we're gonna now use

00:50:44.862 --> 00:50:46.920
across other countries, right?

00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:50.520
Where you can always say,
"Well, how accurate is that

00:50:50.520 --> 00:50:52.737
as an assumption to be made?"

00:50:55.440 --> 00:50:56.520
On the second part.

00:50:56.520 --> 00:50:57.700
Yeah.

00:50:57.700 --> 00:50:59.010
- [Crowd Member] So on the
second part what I was saying

00:50:59.010 --> 00:51:03.240
is that, so you have a path
of the more likely scenario,

00:51:03.240 --> 00:51:07.890
but you also are showing the
results like a probability

00:51:07.890 --> 00:51:09.720
of this event happening.

00:51:09.720 --> 00:51:13.320
So what I'm saying is that given

00:51:13.320 --> 00:51:18.320
that more extremes event
are happening more often,

00:51:18.660 --> 00:51:21.750
are you able as well to build
like different scenarios?

00:51:21.750 --> 00:51:26.750
Okay, I don't know if
temperature rises by this much,

00:51:28.770 --> 00:51:30.840
which I dunno exactly from five

00:51:30.840 --> 00:51:34.173
or whatever figure you
you want to put there,

00:51:35.242 --> 00:51:38.580
this is more likely to be the effect.

00:51:38.580 --> 00:51:41.910
So you will be more like on
the tails of the distribution

00:51:41.910 --> 00:51:46.386
and basically, as I said,
work with the government

00:51:46.386 --> 00:51:51.386
in terms of well trying to
adapt to the different events.

00:51:53.631 --> 00:51:54.840
- Yeah, yeah.

00:51:54.840 --> 00:51:59.840
So we for this exercise that
for scenarios and actually

00:52:00.450 --> 00:52:02.580
in the report we mainly focused

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:05.850
on the kind of pessimistic
scenario in the sense

00:52:05.850 --> 00:52:08.670
that the optimistic
scenario was really we stay

00:52:08.670 --> 00:52:12.480
within 1.5, you know, we are
meeting the Paris targets

00:52:12.480 --> 00:52:17.480
and we said that is not the
most likely outcome, right?

00:52:17.520 --> 00:52:20.280
So we actually have mostly

00:52:20.280 --> 00:52:23.527
in the report focused on the
more pessimistic scenario,

00:52:23.527 --> 00:52:25.890
but then said, "You know,

00:52:25.890 --> 00:52:30.810
even with the more kind of
negative climate trajectory,

00:52:30.810 --> 00:52:33.840
you can see that the development
trajectory matters, right?"

00:52:33.840 --> 00:52:35.910
Like, because there's
basically these two variables.

00:52:35.910 --> 00:52:37.230
How are you doing on development

00:52:37.230 --> 00:52:38.940
and how are you doing on climate?

00:52:38.940 --> 00:52:42.240
And even if climate is not going well,

00:52:42.240 --> 00:52:45.365
if the emissions reductions
are not happening fast,

00:52:45.365 --> 00:52:48.150
whether you make progress
on development makes

00:52:48.150 --> 00:52:50.430
a big difference in terms
of the vulnerability

00:52:50.430 --> 00:52:51.746
of your population.

00:52:51.746 --> 00:52:53.910
And so that's been the main message

00:52:53.910 --> 00:52:56.340
for African governments
basically from the report

00:52:56.340 --> 00:53:01.080
to say like, "You know, you
can not do so much about

00:53:01.080 --> 00:53:02.850
the emissions reductions,

00:53:02.850 --> 00:53:04.260
there's not so much in your power.

00:53:04.260 --> 00:53:06.300
I mean, you can obviously
advocate and so on,

00:53:06.300 --> 00:53:08.910
but at the end of the day
there's not so much again

00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:11.167
to decarbonize in a lot
of these countries,"

00:53:11.167 --> 00:53:14.940
but you can do quite a lot
about the development, well,

00:53:14.940 --> 00:53:17.220
quite a lot you can do something about

00:53:17.220 --> 00:53:18.700
the development trajectory

00:53:19.554 --> 00:53:24.554
and that's where the kind
of difference can be made

00:53:25.350 --> 00:53:26.703
in terms of outcomes.

00:53:29.791 --> 00:53:30.624
- [Crowd Member] So just
to follow up on that,

00:53:30.624 --> 00:53:32.730
so I mean I think you said

00:53:32.730 --> 00:53:35.820
that "Under the rocky road scenario,

00:53:35.820 --> 00:53:37.743
which is the worst case scenario,

00:53:40.500 --> 00:53:42.750
migration actually is slightly lower

00:53:42.750 --> 00:53:45.083
than in a more optimistic scenario,"

00:53:45.083 --> 00:53:47.487
which is an eye-opening point.

00:53:47.487 --> 00:53:48.517
And I think you said

00:53:48.517 --> 00:53:51.510
"It's because that people
won't even have the resources

00:53:51.510 --> 00:53:53.640
that they need in order to migrate,

00:53:53.640 --> 00:53:55.170
which means they're trapped,

00:53:55.170 --> 00:53:58.260
which means things are
much worse in location

00:53:58.260 --> 00:54:00.690
where people aren't, right,
interpreting that right?

00:54:00.690 --> 00:54:02.010
- Yeah.

00:54:02.010 --> 00:54:04.950
And I think from this Caribbean model,

00:54:04.950 --> 00:54:07.380
this comes out even more starkly

00:54:07.380 --> 00:54:11.100
that you'll basically just
have a share of the population

00:54:11.100 --> 00:54:16.100
that's gonna be very poor,
not very likely to be moving,

00:54:16.560 --> 00:54:19.685
especially not across
international borders.

00:54:19.685 --> 00:54:21.660
And that's maybe the people

00:54:21.660 --> 00:54:24.138
that you should be most concerned about.

00:54:24.138 --> 00:54:26.430
But, yeah, I mean we've found that,

00:54:26.430 --> 00:54:29.310
and again, this is our interpretation,

00:54:29.310 --> 00:54:30.930
but I think it is consistent also

00:54:30.930 --> 00:54:33.060
with some of the, you know,
field research findings

00:54:33.060 --> 00:54:35.820
and what we know from the
existing migration research

00:54:35.820 --> 00:54:38.490
that especially going longer distance,

00:54:38.490 --> 00:54:41.040
international is expensive.

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:43.113
You need contacts, you need money.

00:54:44.580 --> 00:54:49.580
And so if people are moving,
they're moving short distances.

00:54:50.143 --> 00:54:53.790
If they don't have those
contacts and those resources,

00:54:53.790 --> 00:54:56.190
they're not moving across the continent,

00:54:56.190 --> 00:54:58.473
let alone to like Europe or anywhere.

