00:57:04.530 --> 00:57:07.151
on the solutions, right?

00:57:07.151 --> 00:57:07.984
Like so far

00:57:07.984 --> 00:57:11.100
a lot of the climate mobility
research has been focused

00:57:11.100 --> 00:57:12.867
on the drivers like understanding

00:57:12.867 --> 00:57:16.408
how important is climate as a
driver of people's movement?

00:57:16.408 --> 00:57:18.510
Where will people leave,
where will they go?

00:57:18.510 --> 00:57:20.910
I think increasingly you wanna shift

00:57:20.910 --> 00:57:24.270
the research agenda also
towards this question of, well,

00:57:24.270 --> 00:57:26.730
what can be done in response

00:57:26.730 --> 00:57:29.373
and what do we know about
things that are working?

00:57:30.660 --> 00:57:32.370
- And I think that that also covers a part

00:57:32.370 --> 00:57:34.080
that was not really part
of this presentation,

00:57:34.080 --> 00:57:35.460
which comes to our flagship programs

00:57:35.460 --> 00:57:38.072
that hopefully is gonna
address some part of that,

00:57:38.072 --> 00:57:39.090
that's gonna focus on green
skills, climate literacy,

00:57:39.090 --> 00:57:40.680
water solution, and data.

00:57:40.680 --> 00:57:42.300
So of course it ties into the research

00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:44.700
that's presented here, but I
think that there's ambition

00:57:44.700 --> 00:57:45.690
and there's also interest

00:57:45.690 --> 00:57:47.640
and also already some efforts going on

00:57:47.640 --> 00:57:50.700
through these programs that's
gonna address hopefully both

00:57:50.700 --> 00:57:54.690
the host communities and people
staying, as Tony mentioned,

00:57:54.690 --> 00:57:58.117
people being trapped or not
having the opportunity to decide

00:57:58.117 --> 00:57:59.400
where they're gonna move.

00:57:59.400 --> 00:58:03.180
So hopefully those parts
will cover social inclusion

00:58:03.180 --> 00:58:05.342
to some degree, 'cause I know
that was part of the question,

00:58:05.342 --> 00:58:06.810
but definitely the economic part

00:58:06.810 --> 00:58:08.310
when it comes to green skills.

00:58:10.020 --> 00:58:10.853
- [Sarah] Yeah.

00:58:10.853 --> 00:58:11.970
- So we've reached five o'clock, so.

00:58:12.946 --> 00:58:13.779
- I have that.

00:58:13.779 --> 00:58:15.810
Yeah, I think we have 15 minutes more,

00:58:15.810 --> 00:58:20.580
but, and I have one question
and then other people

00:58:20.580 --> 00:58:21.480
if they want.

00:58:21.480 --> 00:58:26.480
So what, yeah, I would like to know

00:58:27.750 --> 00:58:32.453
as well how much you include
the risk into your model

00:58:36.030 --> 00:58:39.660
and basically what I'm thinking

00:58:39.660 --> 00:58:44.460
is about people insurance
against these events.

00:58:44.460 --> 00:58:48.360
I understand that probably
in developing countries

00:58:48.360 --> 00:58:53.360
and not everybody is able to
ensure against climate change,

00:58:58.710 --> 00:59:02.310
but I guess working with
the governments actually

00:59:02.310 --> 00:59:07.310
in order to sort of
incorporate the risk of living

00:59:08.580 --> 00:59:11.220
in these areas that you have identified

00:59:11.220 --> 00:59:16.220
as we move from to to
these, yeah, problems.

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:24.000
- Yeah, I think this goes
a bit to what we discussed,

00:59:24.000 --> 00:59:28.907
like how do we get them to
more kind of decision support.

00:59:28.907 --> 00:59:33.030
I mean, I do think we know
that social protection,

00:59:33.030 --> 00:59:38.030
for example, can be
both a means for people

00:59:38.610 --> 00:59:41.370
to stay in places, but
it can also be a means

00:59:41.370 --> 00:59:44.720
for people to move, right,
depending on the context.

00:59:44.720 --> 00:59:49.720
So I think in terms of what
is a appropriate response,

00:59:52.551 --> 00:59:57.540
I guess we want to make
sure that people have agency

00:59:57.540 --> 00:59:58.860
in a certain degree of wellbeing,

00:59:58.860 --> 01:00:01.140
whether they stay or move, right?

01:00:01.140 --> 01:00:05.160
So the intervention I think
whether it's risk insurance

01:00:05.160 --> 01:00:07.424
or it's social protection,

01:00:07.424 --> 01:00:10.920
I think if we are kind of
trying to measure success

01:00:10.920 --> 01:00:13.050
by whether we manage to make people stay

01:00:13.050 --> 01:00:15.780
or have them move might be tricky.

01:00:15.780 --> 01:00:17.940
And I think that's another question

01:00:17.940 --> 01:00:21.240
that is kind of on the
table as we are advocating

01:00:21.240 --> 01:00:23.130
for climate mobility to become part

01:00:23.130 --> 01:00:27.210
of adaptation programming,
adaptation policy,

01:00:27.210 --> 01:00:29.583
how do we measure success?

01:00:30.630 --> 01:00:31.893
What does that look like?

01:00:34.110 --> 01:00:36.030
But yeah, to your point,

01:00:36.030 --> 01:00:40.380
I think it's definitely one
of our action agenda items

01:00:40.380 --> 01:00:41.730
is around this like, you know,

01:00:41.730 --> 01:00:43.800
how do you just basically ensure

01:00:43.800 --> 01:00:46.680
that people can make
decisions about moving

01:00:46.680 --> 01:00:49.440
with, A, having information
about climate risks.

01:00:49.440 --> 01:00:51.090
So this goes to climate literacy,

01:00:51.090 --> 01:00:54.699
but also access to climate services,

01:00:54.699 --> 01:00:58.590
but also how do they have
kind of the resources

01:00:58.590 --> 01:01:02.010
that they would need to be able
to make those decisions, so,

01:01:02.010 --> 01:01:06.240
and then in a lot of cases that
will mean social protection

01:01:06.240 --> 01:01:09.270
or access to climate risk insurance,

01:01:09.270 --> 01:01:12.552
like things that will
actually allow them to have

01:01:12.552 --> 01:01:17.552
a certain degree of agency
in the context of disasters

01:01:18.000 --> 01:01:20.493
and climate impacts.

01:01:23.640 --> 01:01:25.290
- And I think that that
covers some part of it,

01:01:25.290 --> 01:01:27.654
the social part and economic part,

01:01:27.654 --> 01:01:30.527
but I think they're also the
part what culture and heritage

01:01:30.527 --> 01:01:31.360
that we also try to cover,

01:01:31.360 --> 01:01:33.960
but that is not covered
by those factors, yeah,

01:01:33.960 --> 01:01:35.520
for certain communities

01:01:35.520 --> 01:01:36.870
that's something that I
just wanna put opinion

01:01:36.870 --> 01:01:38.580
that we are trying to address as well.

01:01:38.580 --> 01:01:40.320
That in always your question, Theresa,

01:01:40.320 --> 01:01:41.153
but I think that that goes

01:01:41.153 --> 01:01:42.990
beyond this question was asked online

01:01:42.990 --> 01:01:45.780
and this like, it's not only
the social and the economic,

01:01:45.780 --> 01:01:48.969
but also the culture and
heritage of community

01:01:48.969 --> 01:01:50.040
and the community.

01:01:50.040 --> 01:01:51.198
Yeah.

01:01:51.198 --> 01:01:52.950
Hopefully it comes that in
the third phase of things

01:01:52.950 --> 01:01:55.193
to really see what we can
do for communities there.

01:01:59.190 --> 01:02:00.450
- Gentleman there.

01:02:00.450 --> 01:02:01.380
- [Crowd Member] Thank you so much.

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:05.520
This is so interesting
and so many questions,

01:02:05.520 --> 01:02:10.520
but I want to start with one
and could you clarify please,

01:02:12.702 --> 01:02:15.360
when you speak about zones,

01:02:15.360 --> 01:02:18.633
where people tend to migrate,
like green zones mostly,

01:02:20.370 --> 01:02:24.202
are those better off
somehow in any parameters,

01:02:24.202 --> 01:02:25.890
as a result of climate change

01:02:25.890 --> 01:02:28.530
or they're just less worse off?

01:02:28.530 --> 01:02:32.941
And are there any areas
which are better off somehow,

01:02:32.941 --> 01:02:35.460
physically, environmentally

01:02:35.460 --> 01:02:37.320
as a result of climate
change in the continent

01:02:37.320 --> 01:02:39.810
or maybe economically
because of the migration

01:02:39.810 --> 01:02:41.490
and labor somehow

01:02:41.490 --> 01:02:44.070
or the whole continent it would be divorce

01:02:44.070 --> 01:02:46.090
or just a different base?

01:02:48.133 --> 01:02:49.290
- That's a really good question.

01:02:49.290 --> 01:02:50.640
I think you have both.

01:02:50.640 --> 01:02:52.398
So you have actually situations

01:02:52.398 --> 01:02:56.550
where people are basically just
going from one bad situation

01:02:56.550 --> 01:02:59.040
to maybe a less bad situation.

01:02:59.040 --> 01:03:02.880
So, for example, if the results
for Mozambique show quite

01:03:02.880 --> 01:03:05.460
a lot of movement into the coastal areas

01:03:05.460 --> 01:03:07.380
that are we know very vulnerable,

01:03:07.380 --> 01:03:10.320
but because the conditions

01:03:10.320 --> 01:03:14.400
in the rural hinterlands
are predicted to get worse,

01:03:14.400 --> 01:03:18.300
and so the model is
moving people to the coast

01:03:18.300 --> 01:03:20.496
where you have bigger cities,

01:03:20.496 --> 01:03:25.496
but that doesn't mean they're
out of a risky environment.

01:03:26.220 --> 01:03:27.720
But then you also have I think,

01:03:27.720 --> 01:03:29.520
certain areas of the continent

01:03:29.520 --> 01:03:32.730
that could actually
see conditions improve,

01:03:32.730 --> 01:03:35.040
like where crop yields are forecast

01:03:35.040 --> 01:03:37.718
to improve due to more rainfall.

01:03:37.718 --> 01:03:40.830
So you have a little bit of both,

01:03:40.830 --> 01:03:44.250
but I would say very often,

01:03:44.250 --> 01:03:47.520
especially this movement into
urban areas is not necessarily

01:03:47.520 --> 01:03:50.430
a movement into much better conditions,

01:03:50.430 --> 01:03:53.760
especially if urbanization
isn't handled well, right?

01:03:53.760 --> 01:03:54.900
And that goes to your point

01:03:54.900 --> 01:03:56.550
around like economic opportunity.

01:03:56.550 --> 01:04:01.140
I think a lot depends on, again,

01:04:01.140 --> 01:04:03.900
like forward planning
and investment, right,

01:04:03.900 --> 01:04:08.520
to see how can that be absorbed?

01:04:08.520 --> 01:04:11.070
Like we know people will
be moving into this areas,

01:04:11.070 --> 01:04:14.580
can you already put
infrastructure in place now

01:04:14.580 --> 01:04:16.350
that will make it easier afterwards

01:04:16.350 --> 01:04:18.780
to build up housing, for example,

01:04:18.780 --> 01:04:23.780
if you already have the basic
infrastructure in place,

01:04:24.960 --> 01:04:25.860
then it's easier

01:04:25.860 --> 01:04:28.680
rather than trying to
retrofit informal settlements

01:04:28.680 --> 01:04:30.090
that have already sprung up.

01:04:30.090 --> 01:04:31.023
Things like that.

01:04:34.620 --> 01:04:38.640
But, yeah, it's not
necessarily a rosy pick, right?

01:04:38.640 --> 01:04:40.530
Like there's many places
where you're like, "Oh,

01:04:40.530 --> 01:04:42.810
these will be climate havens." (chuckles)

01:04:42.810 --> 01:04:44.190
Yeah.

01:04:44.190 --> 01:04:45.630
- And I think you shouldn't, yeah,

01:04:45.630 --> 01:04:47.400
not be fooled by colors of green and red.

01:04:47.400 --> 01:04:49.950
It doesn't mean that it's
green, it's green light or red.

01:04:49.950 --> 01:04:51.390
It's very simplistic to say,

01:04:51.390 --> 01:04:54.750
but it doesn't cover
that part I think, no?

01:04:54.750 --> 01:04:55.583
- Yeah.

01:04:59.280 --> 01:05:01.023
- [Crowd Member] Just one quick question.

01:05:01.023 --> 01:05:01.856
I know we're running against time,

01:05:01.856 --> 01:05:04.680
but I'm curious around your strategies

01:05:04.680 --> 01:05:06.240
around kind of evidence uptake

01:05:06.240 --> 01:05:11.240
and sort of sensitization
localization of these tools

01:05:11.550 --> 01:05:12.383
and this information.

01:05:12.383 --> 01:05:14.870
Obviously it'll be as
good as whoever uses it

01:05:14.870 --> 01:05:17.610
for some of the planning
and decision making,

01:05:17.610 --> 01:05:19.260
and it strikes me as it's potentially

01:05:19.260 --> 01:05:21.480
a wide array of different policy makers

01:05:21.480 --> 01:05:23.880
who could avail themselves
of this information.

01:05:23.880 --> 01:05:26.430
So, you know, I'm curious
how you're working

01:05:26.430 --> 01:05:29.891
in collaboration with
national subnational actors

01:05:29.891 --> 01:05:33.240
to kind of promote uptake
of this information

01:05:33.240 --> 01:05:36.183
and to promote uptake of the
tools that will be developed.

01:05:37.260 --> 01:05:40.778
- Yeah, so I think right now,

01:05:40.778 --> 01:05:42.570
and David can speak to that more,

01:05:42.570 --> 01:05:46.800
but I think we see it mainly
as an awareness raising tool

01:05:46.800 --> 01:05:48.443
and also as a tool, again,

01:05:48.443 --> 01:05:51.870
to inform this kind of global level,

01:05:51.870 --> 01:05:55.710
more advocacy conversations,
bringing people together,

01:05:55.710 --> 01:05:56.970
starting this conversation,

01:05:56.970 --> 01:05:58.833
putting the topic on the agenda.

01:05:59.910 --> 01:06:03.120
When it comes to the
more policy formulation,

01:06:03.120 --> 01:06:05.730
then it really becomes
this work that I outlined

01:06:05.730 --> 01:06:07.590
with like Uganda, where it's like, "Okay,

01:06:07.590 --> 01:06:09.030
let's work with this government.

01:06:09.030 --> 01:06:11.640
Let's see, you know, how
concretely can we help you

01:06:11.640 --> 01:06:14.130
to make this part of your
planning, like this part

01:06:14.130 --> 01:06:18.180
of your policies, and
how ultimately, you know,

01:06:18.180 --> 01:06:21.870
where we wanna get to is
help Uganda unlock money

01:06:21.870 --> 01:06:24.990
from the Green Climate Fund
to have specific projects

01:06:24.990 --> 01:06:28.290
in place, that's where
we would like to get to

01:06:28.290 --> 01:06:29.607
with these countries."

01:06:31.380 --> 01:06:36.380
And so I think we have kind of
probably different strategies

01:06:36.630 --> 01:06:39.243
with different audiences

01:06:39.243 --> 01:06:42.600
and also still working on
our strategies. (chuckles)

01:06:42.600 --> 01:06:44.190
So that's also true.

01:06:44.190 --> 01:06:45.023
But, yeah.

01:06:46.050 --> 01:06:47.760
- No, I think, yeah, I
think you covered it, Sarah.

01:06:47.760 --> 01:06:49.347
I think it's also part of then, yeah,

01:06:49.347 --> 01:06:51.960
the climate literacy part that
caused by climate literacy.

01:06:51.960 --> 01:06:54.450
But then we also have the
climate mobility literacy part,

01:06:54.450 --> 01:06:56.040
which is, yeah, you can
see the numbers there

01:06:56.040 --> 01:06:57.210
on climate literacy.

01:06:57.210 --> 01:06:59.460
I don't think we're close to
that on the climate mobility,

01:06:59.460 --> 01:07:00.600
trying to merge these agendas

01:07:00.600 --> 01:07:03.270
and have a conversation about it here.

01:07:03.270 --> 01:07:05.640
I don't think that's a necessarily
a conversation in the US

01:07:05.640 --> 01:07:08.160
or in Europe where I'm from,
but less so there even.

01:07:08.160 --> 01:07:12.030
So I think that that's both
on the national, sub-regional,

01:07:12.030 --> 01:07:13.830
but also on the, yeah, looking at COP,

01:07:13.830 --> 01:07:14.983
we talked about COP before.

01:07:14.983 --> 01:07:16.440
I think it's starting to
make its way in there.

01:07:16.440 --> 01:07:17.550
We said we did a pavilion.

01:07:17.550 --> 01:07:20.100
It doesn't mean that it's
front and center negotiations,

01:07:20.100 --> 01:07:22.080
but there are different things
we're trying to do there

01:07:22.080 --> 01:07:23.013
to bring it up.

01:07:24.130 --> 01:07:25.772
Then, yeah, we can talk more about,

01:07:25.772 --> 01:07:26.847
we're trying to do these
flagship programs as well.

01:07:26.847 --> 01:07:28.650
And also working with youths to see like,

01:07:28.650 --> 01:07:30.606
what does this information mean for to you

01:07:30.606 --> 01:07:34.470
and what would it be helpful
to take it and do it?

01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:36.930
And I think that that's
something that, especially youth

01:07:36.930 --> 01:07:39.060
that I work with are kind
of keen to figure out

01:07:39.060 --> 01:07:41.730
with their peers in terms
of, right, now we know this,

01:07:41.730 --> 01:07:44.490
how do we go and take action on this.

01:07:44.490 --> 01:07:46.590
Of course, there's more formal
process with governments

01:07:46.590 --> 01:07:48.120
that we're working at the national level,

01:07:48.120 --> 01:07:51.300
but are also these
constituency-based as you mentioned,

01:07:51.300 --> 01:07:53.220
yeah, other types of organizations.

01:07:53.220 --> 01:07:55.320
There are a lot of youth
that have a lot of interest

01:07:55.320 --> 01:07:58.233
in seeing this is happening
a lot in their communities.

01:07:59.190 --> 01:08:01.020
- I think that where this idea

01:08:01.020 --> 01:08:05.250
of ecosystem building comes in, I mean,

01:08:05.250 --> 01:08:07.620
just looking at the
conversation here in the US

01:08:07.620 --> 01:08:09.973
and how that's evolving
around like, you know,

01:08:09.973 --> 01:08:13.323
climate displacement, climate refugees,

01:08:16.500 --> 01:08:20.910
I think you'll in each
context need kind of people

01:08:20.910 --> 01:08:22.020
that figure out

01:08:22.020 --> 01:08:25.284
what the narrative needs
to look like locally.

01:08:25.284 --> 01:08:27.480
Because I think in most contexts, it's not

01:08:27.480 --> 01:08:30.570
an easy conversation, right,
to talk to people about

01:08:30.570 --> 01:08:33.960
where they might have
to abandon their homes.

01:08:33.960 --> 01:08:36.150
And we've heard quite a few stories

01:08:36.150 --> 01:08:38.550
of people going back to places

01:08:38.550 --> 01:08:40.590
that they were relocated from, right?

01:08:40.590 --> 01:08:43.050
Like where the government said, "Okay,

01:08:43.050 --> 01:08:44.310
Beira is one example."

01:08:44.310 --> 01:08:46.747
We heard the same in Uganda,
where like the government said,

01:08:46.747 --> 01:08:49.200
"Okay, you have keep having landslides,

01:08:49.200 --> 01:08:50.550
we gotta move you out of this area."

01:08:50.550 --> 01:08:53.550
But this area is very fertile for farming.

01:08:53.550 --> 01:08:55.350
So people will be coming back there.

01:08:55.350 --> 01:08:57.900
In Beira, they come back
because they do fishing, right?

01:08:57.900 --> 01:09:01.170
And if you relocate them to the interior,

01:09:01.170 --> 01:09:03.360
that's just not where their livelihood is.

01:09:03.360 --> 01:09:08.360
So I think in practice a lot
of these things are hard to do

01:09:08.941 --> 01:09:13.941
and governments are often not
super keen to take this on

01:09:13.953 --> 01:09:17.430
as a conversation with their people.

01:09:17.430 --> 01:09:20.250
And so I think that's something

01:09:20.250 --> 01:09:25.250
where I think we can
give governments tools,

01:09:25.920 --> 01:09:27.090
but to figure out like,

01:09:27.090 --> 01:09:29.490
how do we talk about this
in a way that resonates

01:09:29.490 --> 01:09:32.250
with our community in a way
that resonates with our people,

01:09:32.250 --> 01:09:34.710
I think that's the work
that they will have to do.

01:09:34.710 --> 01:09:36.691
And where we will also learn, right,

01:09:36.691 --> 01:09:40.023
what does that look like?

01:09:42.360 --> 01:09:43.560
- And I think that
that's also a little bit

01:09:43.560 --> 01:09:44.393
what led us here,

01:09:44.393 --> 01:09:45.870
'cause we talked to Tony last
year trying to figure out

01:09:45.870 --> 01:09:47.191
how to talk about these things.

01:09:47.191 --> 01:09:48.024
(Sarah chuckles)

01:09:48.024 --> 01:09:48.857
So I think that, yeah,

01:09:48.857 --> 01:09:49.770
there are people here that
are trying to figure it out

01:09:49.770 --> 01:09:53.640
for the US and we're we're
determined to figure it out

01:09:53.640 --> 01:09:54.930
to some degree across the world

01:09:54.930 --> 01:09:56.970
when it comes to climate mobility.

01:09:56.970 --> 01:09:59.103
- I think we are working more hands on.

01:10:01.230 --> 01:10:04.320
David mentioned the
Rising Nations Initiative,

01:10:04.320 --> 01:10:06.600
which is also a regional effort,

01:10:06.600 --> 01:10:08.310
but it's slightly differently organized.

01:10:08.310 --> 01:10:10.710
And there it's a much more hands-on effort

01:10:10.710 --> 01:10:12.210
with one particular country,

01:10:12.210 --> 01:10:17.210
which is that is facing
submersion this century.

01:10:17.730 --> 01:10:21.150
And so there it's much more
hands-on thinking about this.

01:10:21.150 --> 01:10:23.880
Like how can the government
go about formulating

01:10:23.880 --> 01:10:26.550
a strategy with the people,

01:10:26.550 --> 01:10:27.960
like with involvement of the people,

01:10:27.960 --> 01:10:28.980
what does that look like?

01:10:28.980 --> 01:10:32.310
How do we on the one hand
keep fighting for our right

01:10:32.310 --> 01:10:36.120
to adapt and to stay while
also making preparations

01:10:36.120 --> 01:10:38.670
for the eventuality that maybe
people will have to move,

01:10:38.670 --> 01:10:40.350
and then what David mentioned,

01:10:40.350 --> 01:10:42.570
what does that mean for
culture and heritage of people?

01:10:42.570 --> 01:10:43.800
How do you preserve that?

01:10:43.800 --> 01:10:46.530
What does it mean for their
statehood and their sovereignty?

01:10:46.530 --> 01:10:50.603
So that's a kind of like
special project, if you will,

01:10:50.603 --> 01:10:52.740
that we are working on.

01:10:52.740 --> 01:10:54.450
And that is getting

01:10:54.450 --> 01:10:57.843
a little bit more directly
into these kinds of questions.

01:10:58.950 --> 01:11:01.320
- Yeah, but tool will be in the pilot.

01:11:01.320 --> 01:11:03.750
But now also it is for
Pacific tools in general,

01:11:03.750 --> 01:11:06.870
but I think it's kind of clear
also when you start talking

01:11:06.870 --> 01:11:08.040
about this Rising Nations Initiative

01:11:08.040 --> 01:11:09.990
that a lot of the things are things

01:11:09.990 --> 01:11:12.150
that people are thinking
about across the world,

01:11:12.150 --> 01:11:14.070
but especially in the
other Pacific countries.

01:11:14.070 --> 01:11:16.020
So even though, yeah,

01:11:16.020 --> 01:11:17.760
the Rising Nations
initiative is spearheaded

01:11:17.760 --> 01:11:20.193
by Tuvalu, Marshall Islands and Kiribati,

01:11:21.630 --> 01:11:22.710
as soon as you start talking about it,

01:11:22.710 --> 01:11:24.780
there are other Pacific
countries that say, "Oh,

01:11:24.780 --> 01:11:27.390
we might not face a merchant,

01:11:27.390 --> 01:11:28.530
but these questions of cultural

01:11:28.530 --> 01:11:29.820
and heritage applies to us as well."

01:11:29.820 --> 01:11:33.690
And I feel like when I talk
to our colleagues in Africa,

01:11:33.690 --> 01:11:35.520
that those are also things that
are starting to come up more

01:11:35.520 --> 01:11:39.210
and more, so even if it's
not existential threats

01:11:39.210 --> 01:11:43.623
to the same degree, there
are questions that remain.

01:11:45.030 --> 01:11:48.663
- Yeah, I think the poetical
urgency is different.

01:11:49.834 --> 01:11:50.667
Yeah.

01:11:52.608 --> 01:11:54.804
- [Crowd Member] Well with
that, thank you very much

01:11:54.804 --> 01:11:55.979
and thank you all. (drowned
out by crowd clapping)

01:11:55.979 --> 01:11:58.097
(crowd claps)

01:11:58.097 --> 01:12:01.278
- Thank you. (chuckles)

01:12:01.278 --> 01:12:03.695
(music)

